Schoenstatt Link Privacy Policy
===============================

Date published: 2016-03-15

What personal data do we potentially collect?
-------------------------------------------------

-   **No IP addresses and no User-Agent strings.** Schoenstatt Link does not
    store either one. Page views, record edits and library loans are all
    recorded without them.

    There is one exception, and it is deliberate: when a page fails, the
    resulting error report keeps a *shortened* address, with the final part
    removed, so that it identifies a network rather than a device. It is what
    lets us tell a fault that affects one visitor from one that affects
    everyone. It is never shown on any page.

    Separately, and outside this application, the web server keeps a standard
    access log in which each request's full address appears for a limited
    period. That log is not part of Schoenstatt Link and is not used to build
    any record about a person.

-   Names, emails, phone numbers for the purpose of facilitating contact
    within the Schoenstatt Movement (no home addresses)

-   Social media contacts

-   Country of identification (not explicitly nationality or
    citizenship)

-   Languages spoken/understood

How do we categorize users according to their access to personal data?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

-   **Page administrators**‒up to 5 close collaborators‒ have complete
    access to data

-   **Movement leaders**

    -   **Institutes and federation members** have access to all
        personal data of the members of their same community
        worldwide, unless otherwise agreed upon by the superiors of
        that institute.

    -   **Members of the General Presidium** have access to the personal
        data of persons in active assignments.

    -   **National and regional leaders**‒movement director, central
        committee members, presidium members and national
        secretary‒have access to the personal data of persons who
        hold/have held assignments within their national boundaries,
        and of the other national/regional movement directors and the
        general presidium with exception of Institute members who no
        longer hold active assignments.

    -   **Diocesan movement moderators** have access to the personal
        data of persons who hold/have held assignments in their same
        diocese with exception of Institute members who no longer hold
        active assignments.

-   **All users with active assignments** have access to the full first
    and last name of all persons.

-   **All other users** have access to the first name and the initials
    of last names of all persons with active assignments. May also
    contact these persons via an online form (without revealing the
    email address of the person to be contacted).

For more information on Schoenstatt Movement organization please refer
the general statutes.

What is this about "assignments" and who can grant them?
--------------------------------------------------------

The subsystem which determines a user's access to personal data involves
four main concepts: Associations, Roles, Assignments and Persons. An
Association can be a Schoenstatt Institute, a Shrine or a book
publisher. Associations can have roles that can be filled by persons.
For example, a Shrine might have a Pastoral Coordinator role. When a
person is connected to an association's role, he is said to hold a
temporal assignment to said role.

As alluded to previously, there is a main hierarchy of associations
which determines who can grant assignments on lower levels. This
hierarchy is composed of 3 levels:

1.  **International level**: General presidium (roles include general
    presidium members, and international coordination; page
    administrators also have permanent authority at this level)

2.  **National level**: National movement associations (roles include
    regional/national movement directors, central committee members,
    and national presidium members)

3.  **Diocesan level**: Diocesan movement associations (roles include the
    diocesan moderators and secretaries)

Users that hold active assignments to the authoritative roles of these
associations have power to modify assignments lower in the hierarchy.

Institute and federation associations have their own internal
hierarchies and do not apply to the abovementioned structure.

Associations that don't fit into this model (for example, book
publishers) can be modified by the national level leaders of that
country, or in the case of an international association, by the
international level.

How long is personal data stored
--------------------------------

All persons have the right to be forgotten and may request this at any
time. Otherwise, contact data — email addresses, phone numbers, web and
social media contacts and postal addresses — is deleted 5 years after the
person's last activity: the end of their last assignment, their last
library loan, or the last change to their contact data, whichever is
latest. Nothing is deleted while an assignment is still active or a
borrowed book is still out. The same applies to the copies of that data in
the record of past edits. IP addresses and User-Agent strings are not
listed here because none are stored; see the first section.

Access to one's own personal data
--------------------------------------

Anyone signed in can download everything Schoenstatt Link holds about
them, as a [JSON](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JSON) file, from *My data*
in the account menu. It includes the person record linked to the account,
its organizational assignments, library loans, the sources the data came
from and the history of changes to it. Anyone without an account can
request the same export by writing to us.

Right to be forgotten
-------------------------

Anyone may request to have their personal data, in part or in whole,
removed from Schoenstatt Link. A complete erasure removes the person
record, their assignments, loans and account, and removes their name from
every change they made. The one exception is the name of someone who is
listed as the author, editor, translator or illustrator of a published
work: the name stays on the work, and everything else about them is
removed. An erasure cannot be carried out while a borrowed book is still
out.

Nothing is kept to recognise an erased person later, so their data could
be added again by someone else. A new request will remove it again.

Telephone number policy
---------------------------

The personal telephone contact number is an especially sensitive piece
of information and deserves special treatment. The following are the
options for publishing the phone number:

-   Allow Schoenstatt Link to store ones phone number if provided by
    another user (default: YES)

-   Show phone number to Schoenstatt movement superiors (specifically in
    the line Diocesan moderator-national movement director-general
    presidium, default: YES)

-   Show phone number to Schoenstatt movement subjects with active
    assignments (also in the line Diocesan moderator-national movement
    director-general presidium, default: YES)

-   Show phone number publically to authenticated users (default: NO)

-   Phone numbers are never shown to unauthenticated users to avoid web
    scraping and being added to spam call lists)

Email contact policy
------------------------

Each user can regulate the following options as to whether to wish to be
contacted via email:

-   I wish Schoenstatt Link to contact me with information regarding
    changes to my personal information (default: YES)

-   I wish to allow any user to send messages via the online form,
    without the email address being divulged (default: YES)

Overseas data exportation
-----------------------------

We may occasionally move data outside of the EEA, but only for backup
and development purposes, never to sell, never to any person outside of
the group of Page administrators.

Ownership and licensing of personal data
----------------------------------------

Schoenstatt Link and its creator own the personal data and reserve all
rights to it. As opposed to the rest of the data which appears on
Schoenstatt Link, personal data should NOT be considered shared under
Creative Commons licensing. (This clause is important for protecting
personal data from lawful distribution outside of the context of
Schoenstatt Link)

Changes to the privacy policy
----------------------------------------

Schoenstatt Link may update its privacy policy from time to time. We encourage users to check back frequently to see changes to the privacy policy. We will mention changes below in the change log so you can look more closely at what has changed. For more important changes we may let you know through other means.


Change log
